; Open the first object and show what is inside
;
; Common part of the open handlers (called from the handlers of doors, chests and
; others, for example #R$A368; the object's own handler may do more after it). The reason
; why the first object cannot be opened (#R$A1F9: locked, or already open) is said with
; #R$A16C ("The door is locked."). Otherwise, when really performing, the object gets the
; open flag (FLAG_ACTIVE). An object with one location (a container, not a door) then
; shows its contents: when it holds something present, #R$A050 prints the line that
; introduces them ("In the chest there is") and #R$9FAF lists them.
@label=OpenObject
c$910E CALL $A1F9          ; can it be opened?
 $9111 JP NZ,$A16C         ; no: say why
 $9114 CALL $9D44          ; only when really performing
@isub=SET FLAG_ACTIVE,(IX+$07)
*$9117 SET 5,(IX+$07)      ; open
 $911B LD A,(IX+$00)       ; number of places of the object
 $911E DEC A               ; more than one (a door): done
 $911F RET NZ              ;
 $9120 LD A,($B6E8)        ; the object
 $9123 CALL $9D97          ; how many present things does it hold?
 $9126 AND A               ;
 $9127 RET Z               ; none: done
 $9128 LD A,($B6E8)        ; the object
 $912B CALL $A050          ; say what it holds
 $912E RET C               ; nothing to list
 $912F LD B,(IX+$10)       ; B = where the object is
 $9132 LD A,($B6E8)        ; the object is the holder
 $9135 JP $9FAF            ; list its contents
